    summary:
      "Can you walk to infinity on the Gaussian primes if every step has bounded length? Basil Gordon asked this in 1962; it has been attributed to Erdős, and Erdős wrote that the answer is almost certainly no. OpenAI's preprint of 26 September (released 6 October) proves the answer is no, in a strong form: for every step bound D there is a number B_D, never computed, such that every connected component of the Gaussian primes joined at distance at most D has at most B_D points. OpenAI's Lean proof (41 modules, importing only Mathlib) compiles on our machine on the three standard axioms, and its statement prints identically to the comparator file the release asks readers to check. SEATS_SUMMARY In four places the Lean takes a different route from the paper. Ours: a Lean proof of Formal Conjectures' erdos_952, which FC marks solved but proves with sorry, derived from OpenAI's theorem with a copy of FC's statement that a script checks word for word, plus a uniform version in FC's vocabulary, and a small exact check of the paper's sieve statement for unit steps. None of this has been reviewed by a human expert.",
    body: `
<p><strong>The problem.</strong> <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/952">Erdős problem #952</a> [Er77c; Er80, p. 114]: "Is there an infinite sequence of distinct Gaussian primes x<sub>1</sub>, x<sub>2</sub>, … such that |x<sub>n+1</sub> − x<sub>n</sub>| ≪ 1?" The page itself says this is not really a problem of Erdős: Erdős recalled that Motzkin told it to him in November 1963 and that it was apparently raised by Basil Gordon and Motzkin, and in [Er80] he wrote that "the answer is almost certainly negative". It is the Gaussian moat problem. When we read the page on 22 October 2026 it had no proof claims, its comment box was suspended, it had last been edited on 8 April 2026, and it did not mention OpenAI's release; Tao's database still lists #952 as open.</p>

<p><strong>The claim.</strong> OpenAI, "Bounded-Step Walks on Gaussian Primes" (dated 26 September 2026, 29 pages, released 6 October as family 28 of github.com/openai/math). Theorem 1.1: for every finite real D there is a finite B<sub>D</sub> such that every connected component of G<sub>D</sub> has at most B<sub>D</sub> vertices. G<sub>D</sub> joins two Gaussian primes when they are at distance at most D, and the primes on the axes count. So every walk through distinct Gaussian primes with steps of length at most D has at most B<sub>D</sub> points, and the answer to #952 is no. The bound is not explicit. The paper reduces Theorem 1.1 (§2, Proposition 2.1) to Theorem 1.2, a finite sieve. For every D ≥ 1 there is a finite set P<sub>D</sub> of rational primes p ≡ 1 (mod 4) such that the set A(P<sub>D</sub>) of Gaussian integers divisible by neither factor π<sub>p</sub>, π̄<sub>p</sub> of any p in P<sub>D</sub> contains no infinite walk with steps ≤ D. Every Gaussian prime that is not an associate of one of the finitely many removed factors lies in A(P<sub>D</sub>). A(P<sub>D</sub>) is periodic modulo Q = ∏p. A periodic set with no infinite walk has no component with more than Q<sup>2</sup> points, since two points of one component that differ by a period would make it invariant under that translation. Adding back the removed factors costs a bounded amount: the paper's bound is max{Q<sup>2</sup>, |E| + (K<sub>D</sub> − 1)|E|Q<sup>2</sup>}.</p>

<p><strong>The argument.</strong> Fix any infinite self-avoiding walk with steps ≤ D, deterministic and arbitrary. Sample times along it and look at the joint distribution of the walk's residues modulo many split Gaussian primes. §4: a stretch of n steps spans a region of area A ≥ cn and has at least c<sub>D</sub>A distinct differences (Lemma 4.1, a degree argument on a torus), and a product of randomly chosen factors π or π̄ over many primes in [T, 2T] almost never divides a nonzero vector in a thin rectangle (Lemma 4.2, via the isoperimetric inequality on the Boolean cube). So choosing an endpoint of a uniformly random difference pushes the residue entropy toward its maximum (Lemma 4.3). §5: a coverage-transfer theorem (5.2) turns near-maximal joint entropy into the statement that the terminal residue modulo most factors of norm p gives every residue mass at least τ/p, outside fewer than p<sup>1−β</sup> exceptions. §6 arranges all this in one schedule of sampling scales, at prime batches T with log T ≈ 100<sup>m</sup>, ending at one common random time t<sub>*</sub>. §7: if the walk avoids zero modulo every selected factor, a short word of L ≈ T<sup>2/5</sup> increments, too short to revisit a residue modulo a prime of norm ≥ T, must reveal at least c<sub>1</sub>/log T nats of information per increment about the residues (Proposition 7.1). §8: those charges telescope, so their sum over all batches is at most log K<sub>D</sub>, the entropy of one increment (Lemma 8.1). But Σ 1/log T over the batches grows like the number of windows, so for one large enough M, depending only on D, no such walk can exist. The paper calls the method "related to Tao's entropy-decrement argument". The heuristic behind it: keeping a walk inside a set of density ρ costs log(1/ρ) per step, here Σ 2/p ≈ Σ 1/log T, and one step has at most log K<sub>D</sub> to spend.</p>

<p><strong>The Lean, and our replay.</strong> OpenAI's Lean is 41 modules (12,703 lines) under <code>OAI/NumberTheory/GaussianMoat/</code>, importing only Mathlib. Its theorem <code>OAI.GaussianMoat.fullMain : MainEndpoint ∧ UniformEndpoint</code> states, first, that no injective sequence of irreducible Gaussian integers has <code>dist (z (t+1) : ℂ) (z t : ℂ) ≤ D</code> for all t, for any real D. Second, for every real D there is B bounding the size of every component of the bounded-step prime graph, which is also proved finite, and the length of every injective finite walk. We compiled the closure at openai/math commit 1581d4c (Lean 4.34.1, Mathlib d13f23b), lake-free and in dependency order, on 22 October: 41 of 41 modules, 0 errors, 7 min 39 s with four workers, peak 6.7 GiB. <code>#print axioms</code> under <code>--trust=0</code> gives exactly <code>propext</code>, <code>Classical.choice</code>, <code>Quot.sound</code>. The theorem and its five definitions (<code>PrimeVertex</code>, <code>primeGraph</code>, <code>component</code>, <code>MainEndpoint</code>, <code>UniformEndpoint</code>) print identically under <code>pp.all</code> in <code>ComparatorChallenges/GaussianMoat.lean</code>, the file the release asks readers to check, which is the theorem with <code>sorry</code>. Our matcher fails on each of seven deliberate corruptions of its inputs (three token changes, a smuggled axiom, a comparator without <code>sorry</code>, a missing block, a missing log), and a planted <code>sorry</code> shows up as <code>sorryAx</code>. No file in the 41 contains <code>sorry</code>, <code>axiom</code>, <code>native_decide</code>, <code>implemented_by</code> or <code>unsafe</code>. The coercion is Mathlib's <code>GaussianInt.toComplex</code> and the distance is Mathlib's metric on ℂ, so the statement is the Euclidean one. Formal Conjectures cites commit adc7f12, OpenAI's initial commit of 6 October; none of the 1,186 files that changed between it and 1581d4c belongs to family 28. LEANCHECKER_SENTENCE</p>

<p><strong>The Lean is not a transcript of the paper.</strong> A seat that read all 41 files against the printed paper found that the formal proof takes a different route in four places. (1) It does not use the prime number theorem in progressions (3.1): it gets a positive proportion of dense dyadic batches of primes ≡ 1 (mod 4) from Mathlib's Dirichlet L-function results. (2) It replaces the torus-degree proof of Lemma 4.1 with a discrete Sperner-type argument. (3) It does not formalise §6.3–6.4 as printed, that is, the ladder of checkpoints A<sub>0</sub>, …, A<sub>J</sub> with β<sub>i</sub> = 2<sup>−i</sup>β<sub>0</sub> and the backward induction of Proposition 6.2. Instead it proves the terminal coverage that §7 consumes from a base case with a constant fraction of exceptions and one transfer step. (4) It fixes the number of windows from D and lets the base scale grow, where the paper lets the number of windows grow. Theorem 1.1 is therefore machine-checked. The printed proofs of Lemma 4.1, of (3.1)'s use, and of Proposition 6.2 rest on reading alone, by our seats and by us. Any hole found there would be a hole in the exposition, not in the theorem. Neither the paper nor the release's scope note (lean/docs/028.md) says so.</p>

<p><strong>The reading.</strong> READING_PARAGRAPH</p>

<p><strong>Formal Conjectures, and the bridge (ours).</strong> Formal Conjectures' <code>Erdos952.erdos_952</code> (FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/952.lean) has been tagged "research solved" since 11 October, citing OpenAI's Lean, but its proof is <code>sorry</code>. It states <code>¬ ∃ (x : ℕ → GaussianInt) (C : ℤ), Function.Injective x ∧ ∀ n, Prime (x n) ∧ (x (n + 1) - x n).norm &lt; C</code>, with steps measured by the integer norm and primality as <code>Prime</code>. We copied that statement into our own file (FC at commit 3b7c94ec, 21 October). A script checks the copy word for word after whitespace normalisation and fails on a one-token mutation. We proved it from the first conjunct of OpenAI's <code>fullMain</code> with D = √C, using a lemma <code>dist (a : ℂ) (b : ℂ) = √(a − b).norm</code>. We also proved a uniform form from the second conjunct: for every C : ℤ there is B : ℕ bounding the length of every injective finite sequence of primes whose consecutive differences have norm &lt; C. Both are on the three standard axioms. The controls: the same file with OpenAI's theorem replaced by <code>sorry</code> reports <code>sorryAx</code>. On two false versions of the statement the proof fails at exactly the step that should break. One version allows steps of norm &lt; C + N(x<sub>n</sub>), which the rational primes ≡ 3 (mod 4) in increasing order satisfy once C is large. The other drops injectivity, which a constant sequence satisfies. The bridge is about ten lines. The mathematics is OpenAI's, and we take no credit for it. It is the first proof of FC's statement we know of.</p>

<p><strong>Theorem 1.2 in miniature (ours, computer-assisted, a sanity check, not a result).</strong> Theorem 1.2 says that sieving by finitely many split primes, with both factors removed, eventually blocks every bounded-step walk. The paper's primes are astronomically large. We asked what tiny sieves do, by an exact computation on the torus (ℤ/Qℤ)<sup>2</sup>. A periodic set has an infinite walk if and only if some cycle on the torus lifts to a nonzero translation. Two independent programs agree on every case: union-find with lift offsets in C, and breadth-first search with lift positions in Python. For unit steps (D = 1), no single split prime below 65 blocks walks. P = {5, 13} (Q = 65) does, and is the smallest modulus that does: its largest component has 580 points. With 17 added (Q = 1105) the largest has 80. For D = √2 and D = 2, none of the 154 split-prime sets with Q ≤ 4,000 blocks walks. Their densities are at least 0.48, and walks percolate. This matches the shape of the theorem: a sieve that blocks longer steps needs many more primes. For the Gaussian primes themselves, D = 1 is trivial anyway: every Gaussian prime except the associates of 1+i has odd coordinate sum, and a unit step changes the parity.</p>

<p><strong>Sources and the world.</strong> WORLD_PARAGRAPH</p>

<p><strong>What this entry is.</strong> The theorem is OpenAI's, and its proof is OpenAI's model's. What is ours: the replay and its controls, the bridge to Formal Conjectures, the miniature computation, and the reading. Labels: the reduction of Theorem 1.1 to Theorem 1.2 (§2) is (a) elementary. Theorem 1.1 itself is (d) computer-assisted: Lean-checked, kernel-replayed here. The paper's printed route through Lemma 4.1, (3.1) and Proposition 6.2 is (c), read but not machine-checked, since the Lean goes around it. The bridge is (d). The miniature is (d). If there is a hole, it is not in the checked statement. It could be in Mathlib or the Lean kernel, which everything here trusts. Or it could be in the paper's §6.3–6.4 and Lemma 4.1 as printed, which the Lean does not follow. A reader who wants the uniform bound B<sub>D</sub> as a number will not find it here or in the paper. No human expert has reviewed any of this.</p>
